4. WLC-vs-KJV cross-check — surfaced two real systemic bugs
============================================================
User requested a deterministic cross-check rather than spot-checking: load both
sides into SQLite and compare per-verse noun counts directly — "select * from
nouns_table where nouncount(wlc) != nouncount(kjv)".

Wrote Greek_Noun_Extraction_NIM/kjv_noun_count_check.py:
  - Installed spaCy's en_core_web_sm model
  - POS-tagged all 23,145 KJV OT verses (English_Bible_KJV/One_Directory_KJV/),
    counting tokens with pos_ in (NOUN, PROPN) per verse
  - Stored results in new table kjv_noun_counts (book_id, chapter, verse, noun_count)
  - Joined against verse_noun_occurrences counts on (book_id, chapter, verse)

First run: 18,100 / 23,145 verses mismatched (78.2%). Investigating the largest-gap
outliers (sorted by |wlc_n - kjv_n|) revealed two real, systemic parsing bugs —
not noise:

  BUG 1 — Aramaic nouns entirely excluded (704 tokens)
    Daniel 2:4b-7:28 and parts of Ezra are written in Biblical Aramaic. OSHB tags
    Aramaic nouns with morph codes like "ANcmsc" (leading 'A' = Aramaic language
    marker). The noun-extraction filter "morph LIKE 'N%'" never matches an
    A-prefixed code, so every Aramaic noun in those passages was silently excluded.
    Confirmed via Dan 5:23 (wlc_n=2, kjv_n=26) — only 2 of ~15+ nouns were tagged.

  BUG 2 — Hebrew nouns with possessive suffixes mis-tagged (~26,000 tokens)
    core_morph() in parse_morphhb_to_db.py resolved compound OSHB morph codes by
    taking segs[-1] (the last '/'-separated segment). For compound codes like
    "HNcmsc/Sp3ms" ("his X" — noun + 3rd-person possessive pronoun suffix, an
    extremely common Hebrew construction), this grabbed the suffix-pronoun code
    "Sp3ms" instead of the noun code "Ncmsc", and the word was stored — and thus
    filtered — as a non-noun. Quantified by re-scanning the raw XML: ~26,288 noun
    tokens across the OT were affected by this exact pattern.

  Combined: ~27,000 missing/mis-tagged noun tokens — roughly 18% under-extraction,
  concentrated in possessive constructions everywhere plus near-total loss across
  Aramaic passages.

============================================================
5. Fix applied and re-verified
============================================================
Rewrote core_morph() in Greek_Noun_Extraction_NIM/parse_morphhb_to_db.py:
  - Strip the leading language marker (H=Hebrew, A=Aramaic) from the whole compound
    string (previously only "H" was stripped, leaving "A" to masquerade as part of
    the first POS segment)
  - Resolve the lexical CORE segment as the FIRST '/'-separated segment whose POS
    letter is one of {N, V, A, P, D} (noun/verb/adjective/pronoun/adverb) — i.e.
    skip particle-prefix codes (C, R, T*) and suffix codes (S*) — rather than
    blindly taking the last segment
  - Verified against every compound-code pattern observed in the 39 XML files
    before re-running: all previously-buggy patterns (HNcmsc/Sp3ms, ANcmsd/Td,
    HR/Ncmsc/Sp3ms, HNp/Sd, etc.) now resolve to the correct noun core; pure-
    particle words (HR, HC, HTo, HR/Sp3ms, etc.) correctly fall through unchanged
    since they contain no lexical-core segment

Re-ran full 39-book parse (--redo):
  - Still 100% aligned, 0 unmatched verse-keys
  - 299,556 total morphology tokens inserted (up from 297,733 — corrected morph
    codes changed nothing about alignment, only about per-token POS classification)

Re-ran noun extraction (--redo):
  - 145,869 noun tokens inserted (up from 119,115 — net +26,754, +22%)
  - 6,007 distinct Hebrew lemmas in nouns (up from 5,568)
  - 0 Strong's numbers without a dictionary entry
  - Internal consistency re-verified: strongs_ot WHERE morph LIKE 'N%' vs
    verse_noun_occurrences -> 0 per-verse mismatches across all OT verses

Re-ran the WLC-vs-KJV cross-check after the fix:
  - Mismatch rate dropped from 78.2% -> 67.5% (15,618 / 23,145 verses)
  - Investigated remaining large-gap cases (2 Chronicles 2:13/14, Daniel 5:23 area)
    and confirmed the residual gap is NOT a defect — it is explained by:
      (a) Versification differences: WLC has 23,213 OT verses vs. KJV's 23,145,
          with cascading chapter-internal shifts (e.g. WLC 2Chr 2:13 content
          equals KJV's 2:14 — a strict (book,chapter,verse) join misaligns
          wholesale through shifted passages)
      (b) Inherent cross-language noun-count variance: one Hebrew noun frequently
          renders as zero, one, or multiple English nouns depending on translation
          choices — exact count parity across languages was never realistic
  - This mirrors the rationale already established for the NT pipeline, which
    settled on a >=95% *coverage* threshold rather than exact count parity
    (verify_noun_coverage_nt_zh.py / validate_zh.py)

